### Link to the code that reproduces this issue

https://github.com/RodrigoTomeES/next-react-compiler-rust-issue

### To Reproduce

1. Create a Next.js app on `16.3.0-canary.78` (App Router, Turbopack) and add `@tanstack/react-table`.
2. Enable both React Compilers in `next.config.ts`:

   ```ts
   import type { NextConfig } from 'next';

   const nextConfig = {
     reactCompiler: true,
     experimental: { turbopackRustReactCompiler: true },
   } satisfies NextConfig;

   export default nextConfig;
   ```

3. Add this client component as `app/page.tsx` — it has a `const table = useReactTable(...)`, a **nested `const table`** that shadows it (inside a `useEffect`), that `table` passed as a **JSX prop**, and a lowercase `<table>` intrinsic:

   ```tsx
   'use client';

   import { type ColumnDef, flexRender, getCoreRowModel, type Table, useReactTable } from '@tanstack/react-table';
   import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

   type Row = { title: string };
   const data: Row[] = [{ title: 'Alice' }, { title: 'Bob' }];
   const COLUMNS: ColumnDef<Row>[] = [{ accessorKey: 'title', header: 'Title', cell: (c) => String(c.getValue()) }];

   function Toolbar({ table }: { table: Table<Row> }) {
     return <button type="button">{table.getAllColumns().length}</button>;
   }

   export default function Page() {
     const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(true);
     useEffect(() => { setIsLoading(false); }, []);

     // ⬇️ THE TRIGGER: a nested `const table` that shadows the `useReactTable` `table` below.
     useEffect(() => { const table = 'seo_articles'; void table; }, []);

     const table = useReactTable({ data, columns: COLUMNS, getCoreRowModel: getCoreRowModel() });

     const toolbar = <Toolbar table={table} />; // `table` passed as a prop

     // Early return so the server renders WITHOUT the <table>; the client renders it after the
     // effect, so the mis-compiled <table_0> is a real element (not reconciled to the server's <table>).
     if (isLoading) return <>{toolbar}<div>loading…</div></>;

     return (
       <>
         {toolbar}
         <table>
           <thead>{table.getHeaderGroups().map((hg) => (<tr key={hg.id}>{hg.headers.map((h) => (<th key={h.id}>{h.isPlaceholder ? null : flexRender(h.column.columnDef.header, h.getContext())}</th>))}</tr>))}</thead>
           <tbody>{table.getRowModel().rows.map((row) => (<tr key={row.id}>{row.getVisibleCells().map((cell) => (<td key={cell.id}>{flexRender(cell.column.columnDef.cell, cell.getContext())}</td>))}</tr>))}</tbody>
         </table>
       </>
     );
   }
   ```

4. Run `next dev`, open `/`, and in the browser console evaluate:

   ```js
   document.getElementsByTagName('table_0').length
   ```

   It returns `1` (the `<table>` intrinsic rendered as `<table_0>`), plus the hydration errors below.

> Rename the nested `const table` (e.g. `const dbTable = 'seo_articles'`) → the value becomes `0` and it renders `<table>`.

### Current vs. Expected behavior

**Expected:** the lowercase `<table>` intrinsic renders as a `<table>` element on both server and client.

**Current:** with `turbopackRustReactCompiler` on, the outer `table` is renamed to `table_0` and the compiler emits the `<table>` **intrinsic** itself as an unknown `<table_0>` element on the client. The server renders `<table>`, so it is a hydration mismatch:

```
The tag <table_0> is unrecognized in this browser. If you meant to render a React component, start its name with an uppercase letter.
In HTML, <thead> cannot be a child of <table_0>. This will cause a hydration error.
In HTML, <tbody> cannot be a child of <table_0>. This will cause a hydration error.
```

A lowercase JSX tag must always be the host string `"table"` and must never be affected by an identifier rename. Removing `experimental.turbopackRustReactCompiler` (Babel `reactCompiler` only) renders `<table>` correctly.

All four ingredients are required — removing any one renders `<table>` correctly (`getElementsByTagName('table_0').length === 0`):
- `const table = useReactTable(...)` (a plain object named `table` does **not** trigger it);
- the nested `const table` **shadow**;
- `table` passed as a **JSX prop** (referencing it, even in a `useMemo`, is not enough);
- an early return (e.g. `if (isLoading) …`) so the server output differs structurally and the client `<table_0>` is not reconciled back to the server's `<table>`.

Note: grepping the compiled client chunk for `table_0` is **not** a reliable signal — a shadowed `const table` always gets a benign SSA rename to `table_0` in the output even when the `<table>` intrinsic is emitted correctly. Only the rendered `<table_0>` element (`document.getElementsByTagName('table_0')`) confirms the bug.

**Not specific to `<table>`.** The same shape reproduces with any lowercase HTML intrinsic whose name matches the shadowed, prop-passed variable — confirmed identically (via `getElementsByTagName('<tag>_0').length === 1`) by renaming the variable/prop/intrinsic in the repro to `section` → renders `<section_0>`, and to `label` → renders `<label_0>`. So the general hazard is any local named like an intrinsic (`data`, `form`, `link`, `title`, `label`, `section`, `table`, …) that is shadowed and passed as a prop, next to that `<intrinsic>` tag.

### Provide environment information

```bash
Operating System:
  Platform: darwin
  Arch: arm64
  Version: Darwin Kernel Version 25.5.0
  Available memory (MB): 32768
  Available CPU cores: 10
Binaries:
  Node: 24.18.0
  npm: 11.16.0
  Yarn: 1.22.19
  pnpm: 10.30.1
Relevant Packages:
  next: 16.3.0-canary.78
  react: 19.2.7
  react-dom: 19.2.7
  typescript: 5.9.2
  @tanstack/react-table: 8.21.3
Next.js Config:
  reactCompiler: true
  experimental.turbopackRustReactCompiler: true


The next info command doesn't work if you have experimental.turbopackRustReactCompiler: true enabled, so I generated the report manually.
```

### Which area(s) are affected? (Select all that apply)

React, Turbopack

### Which stage(s) are affected? (Select all that apply)

next dev (local)

### Additional context

- Confirmed on `next@16.3.0-canary.78`. `next info` reports `16.3.0-canary.79` as newer, but it could not be installed here yet (a registry release-age policy blocks packages younger than 24h) — please retest on the latest canary; the mechanism (SSA rename of a shadowed `table` leaking into the `<table>` intrinsic) should be unchanged.
- Reduced from a real TanStack Table dashboard component where the shadow was an accidental `const table = 'seo_articles'` (a Supabase realtime table name) inside a `useEffect`, next to `const table = useReactTable(...)`.
- Workaround in app code: rename the shadowing variable so it doesn't shadow `table` (e.g. `const dbTable`); or disable `experimental.turbopackRustReactCompiler`.

### Link to the code that reproduces this issue

https://github.com/cluehr/nextjs-scroll-issue-webapp

### To Reproduce

1. Build and start in production mode: `npm i && npm run prod`
2. Open http://localhost:3000.
3. Scroll down to the **Filters** section at the bottom.
4. Tick one colour checkbox — the page scrolls back to the top. ❌

### Current vs. Expected behavior

## Current behaviour
When clicking one of the checkboxes the URL is changed. We use `router.replace` with option `scroll: false` to prevent scrolling to the top of the page and preserve the scroll position. Unfortunately, `scroll: false` gets lost and the page is scrolled to the top.

## Expected behaviour
Next.js 16 should behave the same as Next.js 15 did and respect the `scroll: false` option.

### Provide environment information

```bash
